045417] [17592186066553 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c74308bb-5312-5958-b720-065684db080d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066554 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2x}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066554 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066554 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6f6ed1c-a948-5fcb-b5c7-19b77fe9a489" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066555 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2x^{2}}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066555 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066555 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db754f39-0842-5f57-9957-a5496a275c05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066556 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066556 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066556 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28a7f235-57a0-53cc-a737-590d73e17205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066557 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5x^{2}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066557 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186066557 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7476754f-d6c0-556a-8143-40997a99d8fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068442 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068442 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4z^2}{9}}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd7c79e5-d274-5f04-af44-335b5a6d95df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068442 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac {4z^2} {9}} = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{4z^2}} {\\sqrt{9}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4z^2}}{\\sqrt{3^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4z^2}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{4z^{2}}}{3} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{(2z)^{2}})/(3) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\mid 2z\\begin{vmatrix}}{3} &= \\\\ \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\cdot & z\\end{vmatrix}}{3}|= \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot \\mid z \\mid}{3}|= \\\\ \\frac{2 \\mid z\\end{vmatrix}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068443 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068443 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac {\\sqrt {32x^5}} {\\sqrt{2x^3}}$ given that $x > 0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40af3166-710b-508c-9a8e-2c54365cd238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068443 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {\\sqrt {32x^5}} {\\sqrt{2x^3}} = \\sqrt {\\dfrac {32x^5} {2x^3}} = \\sqrt {16x^2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt {16x^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt {4^2x^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt {(4x)^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\begin{vmatrix}4x & \\\\[5pt] &= & 4 & \\cdot & x & \\\\[5pt] &= 4 \\cdot & x\\end{vmatrix} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $x>0$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4 \\cdot \\mid x \\mid \\\\[5pt] &= 4 \\cdot x \\\\[5pt] &= 4x. \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068444 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068444 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ceac090-9a1c-57d7-a311-f771ece6e0b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068444 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac {45a^4} {5}}= \\sqrt{9a^4}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{9a^{4}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(3a^{2})^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\mid 3a^{2} \\mid &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & \\cdot & a^{2}\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\mid a^{2} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot \\mid a^{2} \\mid &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot a^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= 3a^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068836 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068836 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068836 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068836 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068836 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Squares Under the Radical" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068836 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068836 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bc723e84-3051-5e73-ac57-08da7193c368" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068837 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068837 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying a Radical Expression With Domain Restrictions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068837 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068837 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f03bcc0e-23e1-534b-81bb-146079d37c3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068838 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068838 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068838 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068838 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Radical Expressions With Perfect Fourths Under the Radical" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068838 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068838 #edb/ref