069778 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "57fbfe53-6167-5a8c-ac3b-d190aa02b687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069778 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069779 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069779 #edb/ref 1061 40568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069779 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25606295-3ea3-509a-91f7-294ef43bb1c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069779 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069780 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069780 #edb/ref 1061 40586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069780 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d917615e-b6ee-5929-8832-7bcfe670df91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069780 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069998 #edb/ref 1064 "To simplify radical expressions, we can use the quotient rule for radicals, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\sqrt {a}} {\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac a b}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}} = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{16w^2}} {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression under the radical as a perfect square and taking its square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{16w^{2}}}{5} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{(4w)^{2}})/(5) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\mid 4w\\begin{vmatrix}}{5} &= \\\\ \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & \\cdot & w\\end{vmatrix}}{5}|= \\\\ \\frac{4 \\cdot \\mid w \\mid}{5}|= \\\\ \\frac{4 \\mid w\\end{vmatrix}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069998 #edb/ref 1065 6691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069998 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7bfc07d9-feb0-5bcc-b3f0-50bb42eb2b6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069998 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069999 #edb/ref 1064 "The quotient rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac a b} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{a}}{\\sqrt[3]{b}}\n$$\n\nIn general, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{\\dfrac a b} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt[n]{a}}{\\sqrt[n]{b}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069999 #edb/ref 1065 6692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069999 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a1d735da-05d5-5959-9226-229b7c29c497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069999 #edb/ref 1067 "The Quotient Rule for Cube Roots and Other Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
